File 1: 1_thread_lifecycle.c
Concept: Managing how threads start and stop.

Part A: Joinable Threads (create_joinable_threads)
How it works:

Allocation: It allocates an array tids to store the ID of every thread. You must keep these IDs to wait for them later.

Creation: The loop calls pthread_create. This asks the OS to start a new execution stream.

The Wait: The second loop calls pthread_join. This blocks the Main Thread. It says "I will not move past line X until Thread Y has finished."

How to fill the blanks:

The Function: Replace (void* (*)(void*))sleep with your actual worker function name (e.g., dog_thread or compute_sqrt).

The Argument: Replace (void*)1 with a pointer to your specific argument struct (e.g., &args[i]).

Part B: Detached Threads (manage_dynamic_threads)
How it works:

The Counter: We use mgr.active_threads to track how many workers are currently alive. This acts as our scoreboard.

The Lock (mutex): We lock before incrementing/decrementing because two threads finishing at the exact same nanosecond could corrupt the counter (Race Condition).

Detaching: pthread_detach(t) tells the OS: "When this thread finishes, delete its resources immediately. Don't make a zombie waiting for a join."

The Manual Wait: Since we can't use pthread_join, the Main Thread enters a while loop. It checks the scoreboard every 10ms. If active_threads > 0, it sleeps. If 0, it breaks.

How to fill the blanks:

The Worker: Inside worker_detached, replace usleep(100000) with your actual task logic (e.g., modifying an array).

The Condition: You might need to add a "Stop" flag if you want to kill threads early (like we did with sigint_received in Task 2/3).

File 2: 2_mutex_patterns.c
Concept: Ensuring only one thread touches a specific piece of memory at a time.

Pattern 1: Array of Mutexes
How it works:

Instead of one lock for the whole building (slow), we give every room (array cell) its own lock.

malloc: We allocate memory for n mutex objects.

init: We must initialize every single one in a loop. A mutex is just garbage memory until pthread_mutex_init sets it up.

How to fill the blanks:

Usage: In your main, call setup_mutex_array(n, &ctx.mx_track) after allocating your data array.

Pattern 2: Atomic Swap (Deadlock Prevention)
How it works:

The Problem: Thread A holds Lock 5, wants Lock 10. Thread B holds Lock 10, wants Lock 5. Both wait forever.

The Fix: We enforce a traffic rule: "Always grab the smaller ID first."

first = min(a, b): We calculate which lock is "lower".

By locking mutexes[first] then mutexes[second], it is mathematically impossible to create a cycle (deadlock).

How to fill the blanks:

The Logic: Replace the comment /* ... Perform Swap ... */ with your actual data manipulation (e.g., temp = arr[a]; arr[a] = arr[b]; arr[b] = temp;).

File 3: 3_shared_context.c
Concept: Getting data into threads without Global Variables.

How it works:
The Context (app_ctx): Think of this as "The Database." It holds the heavy data (arrays, large buffers) and the synchronization tools (mutexes). It sits in main's memory.

The Arguments (thread_args): Think of this as "The ID Badge." Every thread gets its own small struct containing:

A pointer back to "The Database" (ctx).

Its personal info (id, seed, start_index).

Pointer Magic: Since all threads receive ctx *, they all point to the exact same array in memory. Changes made by Thread 1 are instantly visible to Thread 2.

How to fill the blanks:
Custom Fields:

If you are doing a Dog Race: Add int *track, int n_dogs to array_context_t.

If you are doing File Processing: Add int file_descriptor to file_context_t.

In Main: You must malloc the thread_args_t array so that args[0] and args[1] exist at different memory addresses.

File 4: 4_concurrent_file_edit.c
Concept: Writing to a file from multiple threads simultaneously without corrupting it.

How it works (pwrite vs write):
Standard write: Uses the file's internal "cursor".

Thread A writes. Cursor moves. Thread B writes.

Race Condition: Thread A reads cursor, Thread B reads cursor, both write to same spot.

Positional pwrite: Ignores the cursor. It says "Write to byte #500 exactly."

This is Thread-Safe provided threads write to different areas.

Calculation: offset = id * size. Thread 0 takes bytes 0-63. Thread 1 takes bytes 64-127.

How to fill the blanks:
Data Generation: Replace snprintf with whatever data you need to save.

Offset Logic: Adjust offset = id * 64 based on your data size. If writing integers, offset = id * sizeof(int).

File Open: In run_file_edit_demo, ensure you open with O_WRONLY.

File 5: 5_concurrent_file_access.c
Concept: Reading a file in parallel (faster processing).

How it works (pread):
Chunking: We divide the file into blocks. Thread 0 gets Block 0, Thread 1 gets Block 1.

pread: Reads from the calculated offset without moving the file pointer. This means Thread 1 doesn't mess up Thread 2's reading position.

Return Value: bytes_read is crucial.

> 0: Success.

0: End of File (EOF).

-1: Error.

How to fill the blanks:
Buffer Size: Change 128 to the size of the record you are reading (e.g., sizeof(struct student)).

Processing: Inside the if (bytes_read > 0) block, add your logic. For example: "Search for a keyword in this chunk" or "Invert the numbers in this chunk."

Summary Checklist for Your Lab
Headers: Always include <pthread.h>, <unistd.h>, <stdlib.h>, <stdio.h>.

Compilation: You must compile with -lpthread.

gcc -std=gnu99 -Wall -o program program.c -lpthread

Globals: Do not use them. Instantiate the struct from File 3 in main and pass pointers.

Clean Up: If you malloc it, free it. If you mutex_init it, mutex_destroy it.